From 6368c4cd35dc1ae34da258423b9a560a2403850c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raiju contributors <raiju@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:55:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: std/wasm: a wall-clock limit per call (timeMs,
 setTimeLimit)

The instruction budget (fuel) bounds a call by the instructions it runs, but a call that is
slow for another reason (a budget set high for a heavy module, an import that takes long)
still holds the thread. A host that runs module code on a request path needs a time bound per
call as well.

instantiate's limits take timeMs, the most wall-clock milliseconds one call may take, and
WasmInstance.setTimeLimit(ms) changes it (0: no limit). A call past it throws "time limit
exceeded", as a call out of fuel throws "instruction limit exceeded", and the instance can be
called again.

runtime/wasm.c keeps the deadline of the call in progress (CLOCK_MONOTONIC) in a global around
wasm_runtime_call_wasm_a; a nested call keeps the earlier deadline. The fast interpreter's
CHECK_INSTRUCTION_LIMIT, which runs on every instruction when metering is on, also counts a
tick and every 65536 instructions compares the clock with the deadline (vendor/wamr/TOV.md
records the change). With no deadline the added cost is one compare. The limit is capped at
one day.

Tests: tests/run/native_wasm.tov gains two checks: a loop with no instruction budget ends
after its 150 ms, and the instance runs again once the limit is lifted.
---
